.TH GITAWAY 1  "gitaway 0.0.0" "gitaway manual"
.SH NAME
gitaway, git\-away \- reach your self\-hosted git server from anywhere
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgitaway\fR [\-\-profile \fIname\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.br
\fBgit away\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
gitaway wraps git. At home it runs git directly. Away from home it will open a VPN tunnel that only gitaway uses, with no system\-wide VPN and no root. The tunnel isn't built yet: away from home, network commands stop with a clear reason.
.PP
gitaway's own commands are listed below. Every other command goes to the git on your PATH, unchanged, and gitaway exits with git's exit code.
.PP
Network commands (clone, fetch, pull, push, ls\-remote, remote update and submodule update) first choose a profile for the remote. If the profile handles the remote's host, gitaway checks whether you're on that profile's home network before running git.
.PP
The same program is installed as gitaway and git\-away. git runs any program named git\-<name> on your PATH for git <name>, so git away push and gitaway push do the same thing.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBprofile\fR
Add, list, show, change and remove profiles. See gitaway\-profile(1).
.TP
\fBuse\fR
Pin the current repository to a profile. See gitaway\-use(1).
.TP
\fBpeer, key, list, tunnel\fR
Reserved for later releases. They report that they aren't available yet.
.TP
\fBanything else\fR
Passed to git unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-profile name\fR
Use this profile for a network command. It must come before any git options.
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Show a short help. git away \-\-help opens this page.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Show gitaway's version.
.PP
git's global options, such as \-C dir and \-c key=value, may come before the command and are passed on to git.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is one place you connect to, such as a home or an office. Each is a TOML file in the profiles directory. For a network command, gitaway uses the first of:
.IP 1. 4
the \-\-profile option;
.IP 2. 4
the GITAWAY_PROFILE environment variable;
.IP 3. 4
git config gitaway.profile, which gitaway use and gitaway clone set;
.IP 4. 4
the only profile whose [hosts] lists the remote's host. If several do, the one whose home check passes; if that doesn't settle it, gitaway stops and asks you to run gitaway use;
.IP 5. 4
default_profile in config.toml.
.PP
If the chosen profile's [hosts] doesn't list the remote's host, the command goes to git without a home check. Pushing to a public forge is unaffected.
.SH "HOME CHECK"
gitaway connects to the SSH server in the profile's [home] section and asks it to prove it holds the pinned host_key, as a normal SSH connection does. Nothing logs in. If it proves it, you're home and git runs directly. A different key, no answer within about a second, or a refused connection all mean you're not home.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gitaway/config.toml\fR
Settings that aren't per profile: default_profile. Without XDG_CONFIG_HOME, ~/.config is used.
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gitaway/profiles/name.toml\fR
One file per profile. Written with mode 600 in a directory with mode 700.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBGITAWAY_PROFILE\fR
The profile to use, unless \-\-profile is given.
.TP
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR
Where the configuration directory lives.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
For a command run by git, git's exit status. Otherwise 0 on success, 1 when gitaway reports an error, and 2 for a usage mistake in one of gitaway's own commands.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
$ gitaway profile add home
$ gitaway clone ssh://git@192.0.2.10:2222/alice/app.git
$ cd app && git away push
$ gitaway use office
.RE
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
gitaway\-profile(1), gitaway\-use(1), git(1), ssh\-keyscan(1)
